1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,829
What if a second sun rose at midnight?

2
00:00:02,869 --> 00:00:04,483
The clock chimes twelve.

3
00:00:04,523 --> 00:00:06,620
The streets are dark and quiet.

4
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:08,274
Then, over the rooftops,

5
00:00:08,314 --> 00:00:11,100
a new glow pushes up like someone turning

6
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:11,996
on the world.

7
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:13,941
Birds panic.

8
00:00:13,981 --> 00:00:16,376
Roosters shout good morning at the moon.

9
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:17,898
Flowers open in the dark,

10
00:00:17,938 --> 00:00:20,515
and sleepy people in pajamas drift outside,

11
00:00:20,555 --> 00:00:23,924
squinting at the brightest midnight they have ever seen.

12
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,511
Then the town adapts.

13
00:00:26,551 --> 00:00:27,845
Picnics at midnight.

14
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:30,246
Skateboarders at one in the morning.

15
00:00:30,286 --> 00:00:31,980
Shops stay open all night,

16
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:34,514
and the owls start a support group for

17
00:00:34,554 --> 00:00:36,181
people who now work days.

18
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:39,412
But the nights stay too short.

19
00:00:39,452 --> 00:00:41,547
Without darkness, nobody sleeps.

20
00:00:41,587 --> 00:00:42,881
People nap in cafes,

21
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:44,415
on buses, and in trees.

22
00:00:44,455 --> 00:00:46,551
The city is bright and cheerful,

23
00:00:46,591 --> 00:00:49,553
but the whole place is also very, very yawny.

24
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:51,928
Astronomers shrug.

25
00:00:51,968 --> 00:00:54,475
A real second sun would roast the planet,

26
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:56,338
so in this thought experiment,

27
00:00:56,378 --> 00:00:57,891
it's a cool, gentle star,

28
00:00:57,931 --> 00:01:00,810
more like a friendly nightlight than a furnace.

29
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:04,113
Everyone gets double shadows, too.

30
00:01:04,153 --> 00:01:06,216
One slides left, one slides right,

31
00:01:06,256 --> 00:01:09,494
and they cross like scissors every noon and midnight.

32
00:01:09,534 --> 00:01:12,277
Kids spend whole afternoons trying to step on

33
00:01:12,317 --> 00:01:13,081
both at once.

34
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:15,384
Here's the twist.

35
00:01:15,424 --> 00:01:17,910
The second sun doesn't rise every night.

36
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:19,615
It only rises when someone,

37
00:01:19,655 --> 00:01:21,509
somewhere, really needs light.

38
00:01:21,549 --> 00:01:24,667
Tonight it's for a little girl afraid of the dark.

39
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:27,309
So the second sun rises,

40
00:01:27,349 --> 00:01:30,494
soft and silver, and the girl falls asleep smiling.

41
00:01:30,534 --> 00:01:33,054
Somewhere a rooster mutters, wrong again.

42
00:01:33,094 --> 00:01:35,053
And the whole sleepy town sighs:

43
00:01:35,093 --> 00:01:37,239
we'll see you tomorrow, little sun.
